Consider two covariant functors $F: \mathscr A \to \mathscr B$ and $G: \mathscr B \to \mathscr A$ that form an adjoint pair $(F, G)$. For every $A \in \mathscr A$ and $B \in \mathscr B$, there exists a natural bijection:

$$\tau_{AB}: \operatorname{Mor}_{\mathscr B}(F(A), B) \to \operatorname{Mor}_{\mathscr A}(A, G(B)).$$

The naturality condition requires that for any morphism $g: B \to B'$ in $\mathscr B$, the following diagram commutes:

$$\require{AMScd}
\begin{CD}
\operatorname{Mor}_{\mathscr A}(A, G(B)) @>Gg^*>> \operatorname{Mor}_{\mathscr A}(A, G(B')) \\
@V\tau_{AB}^{-1}VV @V\tau_{AB'}^{-1}VV \\
\operatorname{Mor}_{\mathscr B}(F(A), B) @>g^*>> \operatorname{Mor}_{\mathscr B}(F(A), B')
\end{CD}$$

Does this diagram correctly illustrate the requirement for naturality when $g: B \to B'$?

